import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Lemma certificates — derivation upgraded from provenance to proof","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Lemma certificates — `derivation` upgraded from provenance to proof","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/lemma-certificates.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/lemma-certificates.md"}'),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/lemma-certificates.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="lemma-certificates-—-derivation-upgraded-from-provenance-to-proof" tabindex="-1">Lemma certificates — <code>derivation</code> upgraded from provenance to proof <a class="header-anchor" href="#lemma-certificates-—-derivation-upgraded-from-provenance-to-proof" aria-label="Permalink to “Lemma certificates — derivation upgraded from provenance to proof”">​</a></h1><p>The axiom/lemma split (<code>axiom-lemma-split.md</code>) recorded each non-kernel rule&#39;s <code>derivation</code>: the names of shipped rules forming ONE measured derivation of the rule&#39;s instance. That field was <em>metadata</em> — a claim that a derivation exists, verified once by <code>--emit-basis</code> and never again. This change materializes it as a <strong>replayable certificate</strong>: saturate the lemma&#39;s instance under the recorded premises alone, reconstruct the positional derivation through the shipped proof machinery, and verify it on real terms, strictly.</p><p>Reproduce:</p><pre><code>.venv/bin/python tools/law_lemma_cert.py            # ~5 s, CPU
.venv/bin/python tools/law_lemma_cert.py --demo silu_mul_form
.venv/bin/python tools/law_lemma_cert.py --with-layout \\
    --probe right_factor_linear
.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_lemma_certificates.py -q
</code></pre><h2 id="_1-the-mechanism-—-reuse-not-new-machinery" tabindex="-1">1. The mechanism — reuse, not new machinery <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-the-mechanism-—-reuse-not-new-machinery" aria-label="Permalink to “1. The mechanism — reuse, not new machinery”">​</a></h2><p>The answer to &quot;can the saturation engine&#39;s witness machinery capture law-level derivations&quot; is <strong>yes, verbatim</strong>. <code>verify_law</code> already interned both sides of a law instance and asked <code>EGraph.certificate</code>/<code>verify_certificate</code> whether the merge replays; the tool simply keeps the <code>Certificate</code> object instead of collapsing it to a bool. The verdict ladder:</p><ul><li><strong>linear</strong> — <code>certificate()</code> replays standalone (strict, no trusted stubs): a genuine step list <code>[(rule, path, lhs, rhs, bindings)]</code>.</li><li><strong>enumerated</strong> — <code>certificate()</code> emits only e-graph-dependent stubs, but <code>all_proofs</code> (bounded BFS over the positional rewrite space) surfaces a linear path, wrapped into a <code>Certificate</code> and verified.</li><li><strong>saturation-only</strong> — merged, no linear derivation within fuel.</li><li><strong>gap</strong> — the premises fail to merge at all: annotation drift.</li></ul><p>New surface, all small: <code>cert_to_data</code> / <code>cert_from_data</code> — the certificate↔JSON codec — in <code>catopt_core/egraph/certs.py</code> (re-exported by <code>catopt_core.egraph</code>), the twin of <code>laws.serialize.law_to_data</code>: terms through <code>term_to_data</code>, bindings tagged <code>{&quot;term&quot;: …}</code> / <code>{&quot;attr&quot;: …}</code> (metavar vs <code>&quot;$attr:&quot;</code> keys), rules referenced <em>by name</em> — hooks are code, data cannot carry them, same honest posture as <code>missing_hooks</code>. <code>tools/law_lemma_cert.py</code> runs the measurement; <code>tests/test_lemma_certificates.py</code> pins it (7 tests).</p><h2 id="_2-the-measurement-—-all-15-materialize-all-one-step" tabindex="-1">2. The measurement — all 15 materialize, all one step <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-the-measurement-—-all-15-materialize-all-one-step" aria-label="Permalink to “2. The measurement — all 15 materialize, all one step”">​</a></h2><p>15 rules carry <code>derivation</code> (13 <code>lemma</code> + 2 <code>redundant</code>). Under premise-only saturation, <strong>all 15 get linear certificates — every one exactly 1 step, exactly the recorded premise</strong> (<code>premise_cover</code>: the replayed step&#39;s rule is the annotation&#39;s name, no extras):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>direction</th><th>count</th><th>who</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>rhs-&gt;lhs</code></td><td>13</td><td>every inverse twin + <code>weight_factor_matmul</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>lhs-&gt;rhs</code></td><td>2</td><td><code>weight_distribute_matmul</code>, <code>silu_mul_form</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The direction column is the finding, not a formality:</p><ul><li><strong>Inverse twins prove <code>rhs-&gt;lhs</code>.</strong> <code>silu_fold</code>&#39;s certificate is <code>silu(x) → mul(x, σ(x))</code> — literally the <em>axiom&#39;s</em> forward step applied to the lemma&#39;s RHS instance. The lemma&#39;s own direction (<code>mul(x,σx) → silu(x)</code>) emits an e-graph-dependent stub: it is <em>unreachable by forward premise rewriting</em> — <code>silu_expand</code> can never produce a <code>silu</code> node. The certificate makes precise what &quot;derivation&quot; means for a twin: the equality is witnessed by the axiom, read symmetrically; the <em>rewrite</em> exists only because the lemma ships. This is <code>silu_fold</code>&#39;s mediation case spelled out — its witness is <code>silu_expand</code>, full stop.</li><li><strong><code>weight_distribute_matmul</code> proves <code>lhs-&gt;rhs</code></strong> — it is the same-direction alpha-duplicate of its premise <code>distribute_matmul_over_add</code>, so the cert is the axiom&#39;s own step on an alpha-equivalent instance. (Its classmate <code>weight_factor_matmul</code> is the factor-direction duplicate, hence <code>rhs-&gt;lhs</code> like the twins.)</li><li><strong><code>silu_mul_form</code> proves <code>lhs-&gt;rhs</code> at path <code>(0,)</code></strong> — the only composite derivation: <code>silu_expand</code> fires <em>inside</em> the <code>mul</code> context, not at the root. The cert step records the non-root path — the one lemma whose proof is more than &quot;the axiom at the root&quot;.</li></ul><p>Nothing is saturation-only and nothing gaps: the annotation table is not just premise names that happen to be sufficient, it is a set of concrete one-step proofs — and the tool now <em>replays</em> each of them on every run. A lemma whose recorded proof stops replaying after a library edit now fails as <code>gap</code>/<code>saturation-only</code> instead of rotting as a stale comment — the drift check the axiom/lemma retro asked for.</p><h2 id="_3-the-honest-boundary-—-unreplayable-refines-to-reconstruction-hard" tabindex="-1">3. The honest boundary — &quot;unreplayable&quot; refines to &quot;reconstruction-hard&quot; <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-the-honest-boundary-—-unreplayable-refines-to-reconstruction-hard" aria-label="Permalink to “3. The honest boundary — &quot;unreplayable&quot; refines to &quot;reconstruction-hard&quot;”">​</a></h2><p><code>--probe NAME</code> runs the same ladder on any rule against <code>universe − {name}</code>. The measured boundary cases:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>probe</th><th>universe</th><th>verdict</th><th>detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>right_factor_linear</code></td><td><code>ALL_RULES</code></td><td><strong>gap</strong></td><td>primitive — consistent with its axiom mark</td></tr><tr><td><code>right_factor_linear</code></td><td><code>+LAYOUT</code></td><td><strong>enumerated</strong></td><td><code>certificate()</code> stubs both directions; <code>all_proofs</code> finds 8 linear derivations, shortest 4 steps</td></tr><tr><td><code>linear_to_matmul_t</code></td><td><code>+LAYOUT</code></td><td><strong>linear</strong></td><td><code>rhs-&gt;lhs</code>, 1 step via <code>linear_from_matmul_t</code> (de-facto twin)</td></tr><tr><td><code>matmul_transpose_rev_bb</code></td><td><code>+LAYOUT</code></td><td><strong>linear</strong></td><td>3 steps: <code>linear_from_matmul_t_bare → linear_to_matmul_t → matmul_transpose_rev_bd</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>comm_add</code></td><td><code>ALL_RULES</code></td><td><strong>gap</strong></td><td>soundness control: primitives don&#39;t self-derive</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>right_factor_linear</code> was the depth-2 retro&#39;s &quot;lone derivable-but-unreplayable law&quot;. The probe refines that verdict: the merge is real AND a linear derivation exists — <code>linear_to_matmul_t</code> at <code>(0,)</code>, <code>linear_to_matmul_t</code> at <code>(1,)</code>, <code>right_factor_matmul</code> at <code>()</code>, <code>linear_from_matmul_t</code> at <code>()</code> — the connect machinery just can&#39;t <em>reconstruct</em> it. The failure is structural: neither endpoint&#39;s root enode is a rule-application RHS (so target-expansion finds no app), the heads differ (<code>add</code> vs <code>linear</code>, so congruence fails), and <code>_edge_path</code>&#39;s fallback searches <strong>root-level rewrites only</strong> — the derivation needs fires at child positions <code>(0,)</code>/<code>(1,)</code>. <code>all_proofs</code> enumerates the positional space and finds it.</p><p>Two consequences worth recording:</p><ul><li><strong>&quot;Saturation-only&quot; is a bound on reconstruction, not on existence.</strong> Under pure rule saturation every merge has some linear witness in principle — each union IS a rule firing, and connect+congruence can compose them. The genuinely non-linear class is non-local-pass merges (<code>pair_shared_input_*</code>, manual <code>union</code> without <code>witness=</code>), which never occur in law verification. The residual honest boundary is <em>budget</em>: a derivation longer than <code>all_proofs</code>&#39; fuel/steps cap would still read saturation-only.</li><li><strong>The enumerated proof is 2nd-generation</strong>: it routes through <code>right_factor_matmul</code>, itself a <em>lemma</em>. A law derivable only through another derivable law is exactly the stratification the depth-2 retro measured (<code>matmul_transpose_rev_*</code> rank-2 chains) — now visible as concrete step sequences, not just premise sets.</li></ul><h2 id="_4-serialization-—-the-store-loop-closes" tabindex="-1">4. Serialization — the store loop closes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-serialization-—-the-store-loop-closes" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Serialization — the store loop closes”">​</a></h2><p><code>cert_to_data</code> makes a certificate the same kind of record a law already is. <code>silu_mul_form</code>&#39;s cert → JSON → <code>cert_from_data</code> → <code>verify_certificate(strict=True)</code> replays to <code>(mul (mul g, (sigmoid g)), u)</code> — demonstrated by <code>--demo</code>. The record carries <code>src</code>/<code>dst</code>/<code>steps[]</code>, each step <code>{rule, path, lhs, rhs, bindings, egraph_dependent, note}</code>; the lemma-store row is now complete: <strong>pattern + cond + derivation + replayable proof</strong>, where <code>derivation</code> is the premise index and the certificate is the checkable artifact. <code>--json</code> emits all 15 certificates as data.</p><h2 id="_5-honest-limits" tabindex="-1">5. Honest limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-honest-limits" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Honest limits”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Everything inherits the instance-level, budgeted posture of the coherence probes: &quot;linear&quot; means one certificate replayed on <em>the law&#39;s registered instance</em>, not a proof of the law in general — though a step sequence that replays on one instance replays on any instance of the same binding shape.</li><li><code>enumerated</code> certificates depend on <code>all_proofs</code> fuel; a deeper derivation could exist beyond <code>max_steps=10</code>/<code>fuel=200k</code> and still read <code>saturation-only</code>.</li><li>Direction is a convention the data makes explicit: a <code>rhs-&gt;lhs</code> cert proves the <em>equality</em>, not the lemma&#39;s rewrite direction — symmetric reading is sound but the record keeps which way the axiom actually fired.</li><li>The codec references rules by name; a record decoded against a rule set missing a name can&#39;t verify (raises &quot;unknown rule&quot;) — hooks, as always, are not data.</li></ul>`,24)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};